{"findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"DESIGN_SCOPE_DISPOSITION","classStatement":"An acceptance criterion adds a fixed-status transition outside the approved design.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC1, lines 63–65, says a later `unsupported` disposition makes a follow-up `fixed`. The approved design, lines 47–48, specifies `resolved` as the disposition that makes it `fixed`; it does not include `unsupported`.","failureScenario":"A later reviewer marks a previously raised finding `unsupported` without a fix -> the spec permits Completion and metrics to report it as fixed, although the approved design does not authorize that transition.","id":"F1","severity":"high"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"AUTHORITY_PRECEDENCE","classStatement":"The spec permits policy defaults to supersede human decisions contrary to the approved authority rule.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3, lines 86–87, allows any later decision on the same ID to supersede an earlier one. The design, lines 66–71, specifically permits a later human decision to supersede a policy default; AC4 does not prevent the reverse.","failureScenario":"A human confirms a decision and a later goal-autonomous call applies defaults to the same ID -> the policy default replaces the human's recorded decision and the run reports the replacement as current.","id":"F2","severity":"high"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"REOPEN_STATUS_AMBIGUITY","classStatement":"The reopened status and its required rubric evidence are undefined.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC1, lines 63–65, says a later `present` disposition makes a `fixed` follow-up 'open again' without defining whether its status becomes `undecided` or a prior decided status. R1, line 160, tests the transition to `fixed` and a peer keeping a finding open, but does not test reopening after `fixed`.","failureScenario":"A finding is marked resolved in one review and present in a later review -> implementations can restore an old decision or require a new one, producing different Completion outcomes while both appear to satisfy the criterion.","id":"F3","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"REASON_PRECEDENCE","classStatement":"Overlapping follow-up reasons lack a testable precedence rule.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC1, lines 56 and 61–62, requires one `reason` value from `low`, `reviewer-non-blocking`, or `demoted`, but gives no precedence when a low finding is also demoted in rounds 4–5. R1, line 160, uses separate examples and does not resolve that overlap.","failureScenario":"An ordinary low-severity finding is raised in round 4 -> both `low` and `demoted` describe it, so two implementations can emit different reason values and both claim to pass R1.","id":"F4","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"RUBRIC_EVENT_COVERAGE","classStatement":"The enforcement rubric omits the required stale-ready check for newly recorded follow-ups.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC6, lines 124–125, requires both `FOLLOW_UP_DECIDED` and `FOLLOW_UP_RECORDED` to stale a published goal ready record. R6, line 165, requires evidence only that a decision makes the ready record stale.","failureScenario":"A goal ready record is published and a non-review gate subsequently records a new follow-up -> an implementation can leave the ready record valid and still pass R6, reporting readiness while a new follow-up is undecided.","id":"F5","severity":"high"}],"mandateAssessment":null,"outcome":"FAIL","summary":"Implementation should not be authorized yet. The spec changes an approved disposition rule, permits a policy default to override a human decision, and leaves ordinary follow-up states ambiguous. The rubric also omits coverage for a required stale-ready transition."}